## Founding & History
Brook Eden Vineyard was established in 1988 by John and Sheila Bezemer. The vineyard is a founding member of the Tamar Wine Tourism Route. In 2015, the vineyard was expanded by Annette and Ed Ferrero, who are the current owners.

## People
The current owners of Brook Eden Vineyard are Annette and Ed Ferrero. They are actively involved in the vineyard and winemaking, and are known to discuss these aspects with visitors at the cellar door.

## Vineyard & Site
The Brook Eden vineyard is situated on a ridge of deep, free-draining basalt soil, also described as gravelly, well-drained, volcanic soil, on a small, north and east-facing hill in the Pipers Brook region of North East Tasmania. The site is considered cool, representing "viticulture on-the-edge" at 41º South, at the start of the famed roaring forties. The vines are unirrigated and experience one of the longest ripening seasons in Australia.

The vineyard comprises four blocks totaling 3 hectares of closely-planted vines. Three blocks were planted in 1988 by the original owners with Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, and Pinot Gris. An additional hectare was planted in early 2015 by Annette and Ed Ferrero with Pinot Noir and Lagrein. Original plantings also included Cabernet Sauvignon and Riesling, with Merlot added in the mid-1990s. Cabernet Sauvignon was replaced with Pinot Gris in 2006, and Riesling vines have since been grafted to Pinot Gris.

Current varietals planted include Pinot Noir (clones D5V12a, G5V15, 0013, 0014, Able, and 115), Chardonnay (clone 8127), Pinot Gris (clone D1V7), and Lagrein. All vines are own-rooted, except for a single row of Semillon that was grafted over to Pinot Noir clone D5V12. The vineyard is trellised to VSP (Vertical Shoot Position) and all vines are cane-pruned by hand.

From 2008 to 2013, the vineyard was managed organically. More recently, it has transitioned to sustainable viticulture, employing low-toxicity herbicides under the vine rows and maintaining mostly organic practices for other vine management needs. Since 2013, over 1400 native trees and shrubs have been planted to create windbreaks and a wildlife corridor connecting the property's wetlands to Pipers Brook.

## Cellar Door Experience
The cellar door at Brook Eden Vineyard is open daily from 11 am to 5 pm, except on Good Friday and Christmas Day. Visitors can expect a tasting of medal-winning wines, with a cost of $10 for tasting seven wines. It is common to meet one of the owners, Ed or Annette, during a visit. Wines can be purchased by the bottle or case, often at cellar-door-only prices, with shipping available across Australia. Visitors are welcome to enjoy a picnic with a glass of wine in the garden or next to the vineyard.

The vineyard is located about half an hour north of Launceston. Specific directions advise taking Shortcut Road off Pipers Brook Road, following signs, and proceeding about 300 meters up the driveway. The cellar door offers disabled access and has a carpark.

## Food & Hospitality
Brook Eden Vineyard offers platters at its cellar door. A commercial kitchen services the cellar door area. Guests can also enjoy a picnic in the garden or by the vineyard with their wine. There is no mention of a full restaurant or extensive menu.

## Awards & Recognition
Brook Eden Vineyard has received several accolades. The 2005 vintage Pinot Noir won the Trophy for Best Pinot Noir at the Tasmanian Wine Show and the Trophy for Best Red Wine of Show. In 2008, their Pinot Noir earned several gold medals. The 2012 Pinot Noir was awarded 96 points by James Halliday. The vineyard was recognized with a 5 Star rating in the James Halliday Australian Wine Compendium in 2016 and received a Lyon Gold Medal in 2021. Their Riesling, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, and Pinot Rosé have also been described as award-winning. The brand is known for its recognizable profile and multiple domestic and international wine awards.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Brook Eden Vineyard practiced organic management from 2008 to 2013. Currently, it employs sustainable viticulture, which includes the use of low-toxicity herbicides under vine rows and predominantly organic practices for other vineyard management. The property is managed with a focus on vine health, nutrition, and the preservation of its natural environment to ensure a sustainable future. Since 2013, over 1400 native trees and shrubs have been planted to serve as windbreaks and establish a wildlife corridor. The property features natural wetlands that are home to platypus.

